WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.084
[MUSIC]

00:00:07.084 --> 00:00:10.814
Welcome, my name is Jules van Binsbergen
and I'm the Nippon Life professor in

00:00:10.814 --> 00:00:13.996
finance at the Wharton School of
the University of Pennsylvania.

00:00:13.996 --> 00:00:18.016
&gt;&gt; And I'm Jonathan Berk,
the AP Giannini Professor of Finance

00:00:18.016 --> 00:00:21.973
at the Graduate School of
Business at Stanford University.

00:00:21.973 --> 00:00:27.254
Welcome to the first episode of our
podcast series, All Else Equal.

00:00:27.254 --> 00:00:30.613
The series is about making
good business decisions.

00:00:30.613 --> 00:00:34.594
We will talk about how to do it and
why it's so important.

00:00:34.594 --> 00:00:38.602
But of course, making good business
decisions is very similar to making good

00:00:38.602 --> 00:00:39.845
decisions in general.

00:00:39.845 --> 00:00:44.339
And so we hope the principles we learn on
this podcast series will apply in your

00:00:44.339 --> 00:00:45.734
personal life as well.

00:00:45.734 --> 00:00:48.980
&gt;&gt; Making good decisions
is a very old problem.

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:53.389
And a major breakthrough on how to do it
properly occurred during the enlightenment

00:00:53.389 --> 00:00:55.849
with the discovery of
the scientific method.

00:00:55.849 --> 00:01:00.167
The scientific method has become so
ingrained that it's hard to imagine

00:01:00.167 --> 00:01:04.216
a world without it, many people
would regard it simply as obvious.

00:01:04.216 --> 00:01:08.190
Of course, major breakthroughs are often
obvious once they're made, but

00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:10.936
discovering them is
an entirely different matter.

00:01:10.936 --> 00:01:15.729
The basis of this podcast series is that
good business decision making is very much

00:01:15.729 --> 00:01:18.030
like applying the scientific method.

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:20.359
&gt;&gt; So what is the scientific method?

00:01:20.359 --> 00:01:26.082
In a nutshell, the scientific method
is evidence-based decision making.

00:01:26.082 --> 00:01:30.931
And the main insight of the scientific
method is that you need evidence to

00:01:30.931 --> 00:01:32.455
support your theory.

00:01:32.455 --> 00:01:36.929
In fact, unless a theory
is supportable by evidence,

00:01:36.929 --> 00:01:41.901
what scientists call testable,
it isn't really a theory.

00:01:41.901 --> 00:01:46.110
Scientists don't waste time and
effort on theories that are untestable.

00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:50.844
&gt;&gt; One other thing that is important from
the business perspective is that we would

00:01:50.844 --> 00:01:55.663
like to reject theories and we would like
to reject ideas as quickly as possible.

00:01:55.663 --> 00:01:59.783
And the reason why it is important as
a business decision to try to do this,

00:01:59.783 --> 00:02:03.444
that we don't want to waste
resources on things that don't work.

00:02:03.444 --> 00:02:08.282
And so it's important that we organize
our organizations in such a way that we

00:02:08.282 --> 00:02:12.685
give people the opportunity to go
after things and try to reject them.

00:02:12.685 --> 00:02:14.906
Now, here's the issue.

00:02:14.906 --> 00:02:16.341
For most people,

00:02:16.341 --> 00:02:22.092
it feels much better to be proven right
than it feels to be proven wrong.

00:02:22.092 --> 00:02:25.116
Now, it's hard to say why that
is because you could say,

00:02:25.116 --> 00:02:28.328
if you're proven wrong you've
learned something new, and

00:02:28.328 --> 00:02:31.304
shouldn't learning something
new make you feel good?

00:02:31.304 --> 00:02:34.821
For some reason for most people,
it doesn't work that way.

00:02:34.821 --> 00:02:38.500
It feels that if you're proven wrong,
that you've lost something.

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:42.812
And so for that reason, we need to set
up rules and we need to set up a system,

00:02:42.812 --> 00:02:45.755
which is really what
the scientific method did, so

00:02:45.755 --> 00:02:48.505
that we can together try
to overcome that flaw.

00:02:48.505 --> 00:02:53.377
So in group decision making and making
decisions together, we would like to set

00:02:53.377 --> 00:02:58.176
up things in such a way that this human
flaw of not wanting to admit your mistakes

00:02:58.176 --> 00:03:02.781
and sticking to your guns is overcome so
that we together can move forward.

00:03:02.781 --> 00:03:07.888
&gt;&gt; Yeah, it reminds me of an anecdote
attributed to Alfred Sloan,

00:03:07.888 --> 00:03:10.923
the legendary CEO of General Motors.

00:03:10.923 --> 00:03:15.649
He was chairing a board meeting where
a major decision was being made.

00:03:15.649 --> 00:03:21.907
And he turned around and asked for dissent
on the decision, and nobody dissented.

00:03:21.907 --> 00:03:27.313
When that happened, he decided to table
the decision and he said to everybody,

00:03:27.313 --> 00:03:31.520
we cannot make a decision of
this magnitude without dissent.

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:36.501
And the key there is,
if you don't try, you can't falsify.

00:03:36.501 --> 00:03:41.171
So a common mistake for
people to make is they don't try.

00:03:41.171 --> 00:03:45.707
This is a very common mistake
in business organizations.

00:03:45.707 --> 00:03:50.923
As the famous physicist Richard Feynman
once said, the first principle

00:03:50.923 --> 00:03:55.980
is you must not fool yourself, and
you are the easiest person to fool.

00:03:55.980 --> 00:03:58.460
&gt;&gt; And in many organizations,
it's even worse than that.

00:03:58.460 --> 00:04:01.443
It is that the organizations sometimes or

00:04:01.443 --> 00:04:05.971
maybe even often is set up that
falsifying things is punished.

00:04:05.971 --> 00:04:10.684
If you prove something or somebody wrong,
particularly somebody that is higher up in

00:04:10.684 --> 00:04:14.489
the organization, it doesn't often
lead to big career successes.

00:04:14.489 --> 00:04:19.453
And so one way of improving organizational
structures is to make sure that

00:04:19.453 --> 00:04:22.818
if an important idea is
convincingly falsified,

00:04:22.818 --> 00:04:26.840
that the people who falsify it
are being rewarded for that.

00:04:26.840 --> 00:04:30.999
Now, one way to institutionalize
things is to set up explicitly

00:04:30.999 --> 00:04:33.702
what's called Red Teams and Blue Teams.

00:04:33.702 --> 00:04:38.247
Where you intentionally assign people
on opposite sides of an argument so

00:04:38.247 --> 00:04:42.571
that you try to get all the pros and
cons of the decision out in the open,

00:04:42.571 --> 00:04:46.917
so that everybody can hear about them and
trade them off accordingly.

00:04:46.917 --> 00:04:52.945
&gt;&gt; In science, one of the best examples
of a cost of trying not to falsify or

00:04:52.945 --> 00:04:57.569
worse having an organization
that punishes people for

00:04:57.569 --> 00:05:02.712
dissent, that punishes people for
falsifying is Galileo.

00:05:02.712 --> 00:05:07.029
Before Galileo's time,
the center of the Renaissance,

00:05:07.029 --> 00:05:10.472
the center of
the Enlightenment was in Italy.

00:05:10.472 --> 00:05:14.579
And then Galileo came
around with hard evidence,

00:05:14.579 --> 00:05:18.988
that the universe does not
revolve around the Earth.

00:05:18.988 --> 00:05:22.719
Rather than look at that evidence,
what the Church did was shut him down.

00:05:22.719 --> 00:05:27.493
And in shutting him down and in punishing
him, wasn't just they shut him down,

00:05:27.493 --> 00:05:28.713
they punished him.

00:05:28.713 --> 00:05:33.558
They sent this clear message that they
were not in the falsifiable business.

00:05:33.558 --> 00:05:38.150
And the net result was that the
Enlightenment moved to Northern Europe,

00:05:38.150 --> 00:05:41.017
outside the purview of
the Catholic Church.

00:05:41.017 --> 00:05:45.187
And so all major breakthroughs,
previously was in Italy and

00:05:45.187 --> 00:05:47.899
after that, it was not in Italy again.

00:05:47.899 --> 00:05:52.916
So the cost to the intellectual
environment in Italy was enormous.

00:05:52.916 --> 00:05:53.561
&gt;&gt; Now, and so

00:05:53.561 --> 00:05:57.441
the Enlightenment values really
stressed the believe that science and

00:05:57.441 --> 00:06:02.238
logic give individuals more knowledge and
understanding then tradition or feelings.

00:06:02.238 --> 00:06:05.907
And so in that sense,
it's closely related to rationalism.

00:06:05.907 --> 00:06:10.462
So now if you wanted to get some sense of
how successful the scientific method has

00:06:10.462 --> 00:06:13.386
been, it is no coincidence that,
if you go back for

00:06:13.386 --> 00:06:17.806
as long as we have decent data in
economic growth that say between 1300 and

00:06:17.806 --> 00:06:21.615
1800 we had a 500 years spell
of almost no economic growth.

00:06:21.615 --> 00:06:25.759
And then in 1800s, suddenly, there was
this explosion of economic growth,

00:06:25.759 --> 00:06:27.268
it was almost like a miracle.

00:06:27.268 --> 00:06:31.666
And it's not a coincidence that that
moment of economic growth arising

00:06:31.666 --> 00:06:34.819
coincides with the introduction
of the scientific

00:06:34.819 --> 00:06:37.326
method through enlightenment values.

00:06:37.326 --> 00:06:42.065
&gt;&gt; Jules, I think it's hard
not to argue that Google isn't

00:06:42.065 --> 00:06:46.029
the most successful
organization of our time.

00:06:46.029 --> 00:06:48.828
And I think, looking from the outside,

00:06:48.828 --> 00:06:54.508
that success is very largely driven by
the fact that they adopt these principles,

00:06:54.508 --> 00:06:59.223
that they have a single focus on
evidence-based decision making.

00:06:59.223 --> 00:07:04.448
And essentially, applying the scientific
method to making business decisions.

00:07:04.448 --> 00:07:10.627
So we are really happy to have on our
show, the Chief Financial Officer

00:07:10.627 --> 00:07:15.784
of Alphabet,
the parent company of Google, Ruth Porat.

00:07:15.784 --> 00:07:18.658
Ruth, welcome to our show.

00:07:18.658 --> 00:07:21.191
&gt;&gt; I am delighted to be with you,
thanks for having me.

00:07:21.191 --> 00:07:23.625
&gt;&gt; Ruth, we are so
excited that you're with us today.

00:07:23.625 --> 00:07:26.097
So let me start by
setting the stage a bit.

00:07:26.097 --> 00:07:29.400
Right now your company's
stock market capitalization

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:33.699
is close to $2 trillion with cash
holdings close to $140 billion.

00:07:33.699 --> 00:07:37.996
Just in 2020, you made $12 billion in
investments in plant, property, and

00:07:37.996 --> 00:07:38.671
equipment.

00:07:38.671 --> 00:07:42.158
And you've also done some major
acquisitions in the past few years.

00:07:42.158 --> 00:07:45.907
One example is Fitbit,
which you acquired for over $2 billion.

00:07:45.907 --> 00:07:49.514
These types of investment decisions
are only a small subset of the business

00:07:49.514 --> 00:07:51.990
decisions that you need to
make on a regular basis.

00:07:51.990 --> 00:07:55.379
So that really brings us to
the main theme of this podcast,

00:07:55.379 --> 00:07:58.296
which is how do you make
good business decisions?

00:07:58.296 --> 00:08:01.786
And I don't think we could think of a
better guest to kick to podcast off with.

00:08:01.786 --> 00:08:04.274
So let me pose this
question directly to you,

00:08:04.274 --> 00:08:08.598
how does Google make major business
decisions and what process do you follow?

00:08:08.598 --> 00:08:12.221
Maybe you could also comment a bit on
what you would say is Google's biggest

00:08:12.221 --> 00:08:13.150
strength in this.

00:08:13.150 --> 00:08:18.146
&gt;&gt; Well, I'd say one of the core processes
at Google that I greatly admire is

00:08:18.146 --> 00:08:20.168
what we call the OKR process.

00:08:20.168 --> 00:08:24.907
On an annual basis, every product area,
every function then rolling up at

00:08:24.907 --> 00:08:30.420
the corporate level, we set objectives,
the O, and establish key results, the KR.

00:08:30.420 --> 00:08:35.761
And that becomes a heavily discussed,
debated sort of strategic,

00:08:35.761 --> 00:08:39.145
set the priorities for the upcoming year.

00:08:39.145 --> 00:08:43.574
And the goal with OKRs is one
that they're audacious enough so

00:08:43.574 --> 00:08:48.273
that by the end of the year,
you achieve about two thirds of them.

00:08:48.273 --> 00:08:50.876
The goal is not to achieve 100%.

00:08:50.876 --> 00:08:55.933
The view is if you achieve 100%, you have
not set the goals high enough and then

00:08:55.933 --> 00:09:01.079
have very specific metrics that where you
can measure your progress along the way.

00:09:01.079 --> 00:09:02.866
And there's quite a bit of debate and

00:09:02.866 --> 00:09:05.404
discussion to set what is
that strategic roadmap and

00:09:05.404 --> 00:09:08.761
then to roll it up so that there's
a true north for the whole company.

00:09:08.761 --> 00:09:11.460
And there's a shared point of
view about what we're doing.

00:09:11.460 --> 00:09:13.380
That's the starting point.

00:09:13.380 --> 00:09:17.320
&gt;&gt; It sounds to me that this process
is also very data analytical process.

00:09:17.320 --> 00:09:21.130
So that raises the question,
how important is data to you?

00:09:21.130 --> 00:09:24.057
And how do you deal with people
that would say things like,

00:09:24.057 --> 00:09:27.128
I think that decision making
should be done at the gut level?

00:09:27.128 --> 00:09:30.891
If it feels right to me, I want to go for
it regardless of what the data says,

00:09:30.891 --> 00:09:32.977
how do you respond when people say that?

00:09:32.977 --> 00:09:37.340
&gt;&gt; Well you've hit a couple of points that
I think are most important, I've said so

00:09:37.340 --> 00:09:40.625
often, anchor everything in data and
the rest will follow.

00:09:40.625 --> 00:09:44.971
You absolutely need to have the truth that
comes from the data and the analytics.

00:09:44.971 --> 00:09:48.719
I think the biggest mistake is when
people think that because they've

00:09:48.719 --> 00:09:51.650
done it before and it worked,
they can operate on gut.

00:09:51.650 --> 00:09:56.453
And frankly, I would say if you're
concerned about taking the time to do

00:09:56.453 --> 00:09:58.748
the data and the analytics, why?

00:09:58.748 --> 00:10:03.377
It should be reinforcing to a decision and
in fact through the OKR process.

00:10:03.377 --> 00:10:08.453
And frankly, everything that we do,
it helps lay out what is that roadmap?

00:10:08.453 --> 00:10:13.435
What are the metrics and milestones along
the way where you can gauge whether you're

00:10:13.435 --> 00:10:15.833
making the progress that you expected?

00:10:15.833 --> 00:10:19.480
And if you aren't, maybe the reason
is you need to invest more.

00:10:19.480 --> 00:10:23.381
Maybe the reason is that
the idea wasn't as audacious or

00:10:23.381 --> 00:10:28.620
well thought out as needed, but
data are as referred the arbiter of truth.

00:10:28.620 --> 00:10:33.328
And so A, critically important, I would
say the other elements of your question

00:10:33.328 --> 00:10:38.123
that's really important is when you're
thinking about, what are the metrics?

00:10:38.123 --> 00:10:39.995
What data do you want?

00:10:39.995 --> 00:10:45.566
That is not always as obvious as it seems.

00:10:45.566 --> 00:10:50.172
You need to make sure that
the metrics that are being selected

00:10:50.172 --> 00:10:54.510
are ones that are driving
the outcome that is desired.

00:10:54.510 --> 00:10:59.269
And I think we can all think about
companies over history who've selected

00:10:59.269 --> 00:11:02.350
the wrong metrics and
set the wrong northstar.

00:11:02.350 --> 00:11:05.340
So that data and
the choice of metrics is key.

00:11:05.340 --> 00:11:11.490
&gt;&gt; So Ruth, speaking as a professor, often
people don't like what the data says.

00:11:11.490 --> 00:11:15.580
They come in with emotions, they come
in with a set way of doing things.

00:11:16.620 --> 00:11:20.300
The data says something different,
and they ignore the data.

00:11:20.300 --> 00:11:22.334
How do you deal with that?

00:11:22.334 --> 00:11:27.316
&gt;&gt; Well, again, I think one of the really
important elements in data and

00:11:27.316 --> 00:11:31.708
analytics is to look at the trends
that come through the data,

00:11:31.708 --> 00:11:35.604
I think that what you don't
want is a false precision.

00:11:35.604 --> 00:11:40.224
I often say, I don't like the numbers, I
like the trends and I like the sensitivity

00:11:40.224 --> 00:11:44.250
analysis because I think it is too easy
to anchor on just a set of numbers.

00:11:44.250 --> 00:11:46.010
That in my view is not helpful.

00:11:46.010 --> 00:11:49.770
The most important is to look at
what are the trends over time.

00:11:49.770 --> 00:11:54.270
And then to your question,
break it down to the sensitivity analysis.

00:11:54.270 --> 00:11:58.530
What are the key variables that will drive
behavior one way or another or outcomes?

00:11:58.530 --> 00:12:00.192
What must you believe?

00:12:00.192 --> 00:12:04.862
And when you lay out in my view
the sensitivity analysis and

00:12:04.862 --> 00:12:10.199
you engage in a discussion about
the variables that will drive it,

00:12:10.199 --> 00:12:13.646
it makes for a more constructive process.

00:12:13.646 --> 00:12:18.142
Coming with just a kind of flat set
of data isn't constructive because

00:12:18.142 --> 00:12:21.428
obviously the world in which
we live is not static.

00:12:21.428 --> 00:12:26.149
And so there may be some validity
in the point that there's concern

00:12:26.149 --> 00:12:28.941
about what a flat set of data tells you.

00:12:28.941 --> 00:12:33.652
And hence the importance in
sensitivity analysis and engaging your

00:12:33.652 --> 00:12:38.621
business partner, your colleagues
with what do you disagree and why.

00:12:38.621 --> 00:12:41.440
And then trying to break
down those elements of it.

00:12:41.440 --> 00:12:45.999
But to me the sensitivity analysis is
one of the key tools that we all use.

00:12:45.999 --> 00:12:47.420
&gt;&gt; Very nice.

00:12:47.420 --> 00:12:50.925
So one of the big theme that we want to
address in this podcast is what

00:12:50.925 --> 00:12:54.712
are the most common mistakes that
people make when making decisions?

00:12:54.712 --> 00:12:58.536
What do you think those mistakes are and
what can you do about them?

00:12:58.536 --> 00:13:02.566
&gt;&gt; Well, you both already hit on the
biggest mistake and I most certainly saw

00:13:02.566 --> 00:13:06.596
it as a banker and that's when people
decide that they can go on instinct or

00:13:06.596 --> 00:13:09.004
their gut as opposed to data and
analytics.

00:13:09.004 --> 00:13:13.130
As I've already said, that really does
not happen with any frequency at Google.

00:13:13.130 --> 00:13:16.576
I think the other critical
element is an over reliance on

00:13:16.576 --> 00:13:19.214
what I've described as sort of flat data,

00:13:19.214 --> 00:13:23.346
not looking at the dynamic nature
of the world in which we operate.

00:13:23.346 --> 00:13:27.818
And that's the value of sensitivity
analysis, but it's also the importance of

00:13:27.818 --> 00:13:32.026
understanding, when you're living in
a very competitive market as we do,

00:13:32.026 --> 00:13:36.102
that any action we take is going to lead
to change in that overall ecosystem,

00:13:36.102 --> 00:13:39.818
and trying to think through how
the world will continue to evolve.

00:13:39.818 --> 00:13:43.659
And so sort of living in a silo,
living in a cave and

00:13:43.659 --> 00:13:49.438
not understanding the dynamic nature
of what one does I think is a key area.

00:13:49.438 --> 00:13:50.887
And then I think the third and

00:13:50.887 --> 00:13:55.057
it goes to the importance of setting out
goals upfront and having clear metrics and

00:13:55.057 --> 00:13:58.460
milestones against which you
measure your performance.

00:13:58.460 --> 00:14:02.697
You have to have a way to check along
the way whether things are working, and

00:14:02.697 --> 00:14:04.800
you have to admit when it's not.

00:14:04.800 --> 00:14:07.680
And I would say that if you
don't have things that fail,

00:14:07.680 --> 00:14:11.851
you're either not reaching out high
enough, or you're not being honest enough

00:14:11.851 --> 00:14:15.060
with yourself that you're not
doing as well as you expected.

00:14:15.060 --> 00:14:20.052
And so there's a whole protocol at Google
around what's called blameless post

00:14:20.052 --> 00:14:21.310
mortems.

00:14:21.310 --> 00:14:25.640
It's the goal to actually do a root
analysis at the end of something that

00:14:25.640 --> 00:14:28.580
hasn't gone as well as you want.

00:14:28.580 --> 00:14:35.320
Not blaming anyone assuming that process
actually can lead to bad outcomes.

00:14:35.320 --> 00:14:38.320
People have good intent, but
you need the right process.

00:14:38.320 --> 00:14:43.421
So what is it that we can learn through
a blameless post mortem, such that next

00:14:43.421 --> 00:14:48.218
time you're in a similar situation
you actually have a better outcome?

00:14:48.218 --> 00:14:52.602
And I'm very much of the view that one of
the most valuable things each of us brings

00:14:52.602 --> 00:14:56.795
is pattern recognition from some of
the toughest things that we've ever gone

00:14:56.795 --> 00:14:57.391
through.

00:14:57.391 --> 00:15:01.739
But you need to take the time to
deconstruct, what did I learn, what

00:15:01.739 --> 00:15:07.105
pattern recognition is important, so that
it sets you up better for the next time.

00:15:07.105 --> 00:15:10.580
&gt;&gt; This is an important theme
in this podcast series.

00:15:10.580 --> 00:15:14.230
It's very important that
you've made your mistakes.

00:15:14.230 --> 00:15:17.102
And so why is that so
hard to do, when obviously,

00:15:17.102 --> 00:15:20.440
you learn the most from
when you make a mistake.

00:15:20.440 --> 00:15:24.410
I mean, in principle, it should be
easy to admit you make the mistake.

00:15:24.410 --> 00:15:25.488
So in that vein,

00:15:25.488 --> 00:15:30.960
what mistakes have you made yourself
that you particularly watch out for?

00:15:30.960 --> 00:15:33.700
What are you most careful about not doing?

00:15:34.870 --> 00:15:36.821
&gt;&gt; I'd like to actually
comment on the first part and

00:15:36.821 --> 00:15:38.530
then I will come to the second part.

00:15:38.530 --> 00:15:42.610
You're absolutely right that human nature
is it can be hard even when you're trying

00:15:42.610 --> 00:15:45.540
to make it safe to say,
something went wrong.

00:15:45.540 --> 00:15:53.475
And probably one of the best approaches
I've seen is at X, our moonshot factory,

00:15:53.475 --> 00:15:59.137
where they actually celebrate When
they're duplicating something,

00:15:59.137 --> 00:16:03.276
because they want to make it safe to
say I tried, I reached, it didn't work.

00:16:03.276 --> 00:16:06.794
And if you don't celebrate and
say I learned something from it,

00:16:06.794 --> 00:16:09.937
it's going to make it hard for
people to raise their hands.

00:16:09.937 --> 00:16:13.975
I created something in finance,
similarly I call it the super duck report,

00:16:13.975 --> 00:16:18.282
which is I want people to raise their
hands early when something's not working.

00:16:18.282 --> 00:16:22.316
Because my view is that when
we bring people together,

00:16:22.316 --> 00:16:27.414
we can solve problems and time is
the one thing you can never get back.

00:16:27.414 --> 00:16:31.042
And so you have to find ways
to actually make it fun and

00:16:31.042 --> 00:16:35.836
safe to say something seems like
it's not going as well as expected.

00:16:35.836 --> 00:16:37.572
So I do think that's a critical area.

00:16:37.572 --> 00:16:43.587
To your question, where have I
found that I keep going back to it.

00:16:43.587 --> 00:16:48.459
I would say probably the most
important thing as leaders is we need

00:16:48.459 --> 00:16:53.433
to have the right organizational
structure and the right people.

00:16:53.433 --> 00:16:54.839
&gt;&gt; Yes, absolutely.

00:16:54.839 --> 00:16:59.170
And there was something earlier that
you said that I also thought was very

00:16:59.170 --> 00:17:00.095
interesting.

00:17:00.095 --> 00:17:03.381
Because one thing that we wanted to push
as an idea is that good business decision

00:17:03.381 --> 00:17:05.403
making is very much like
the scientific method.

00:17:05.403 --> 00:17:08.130
So quickly finding out
what doesn't work so

00:17:08.130 --> 00:17:11.309
that everybody can move
forward is very valuable.

00:17:11.309 --> 00:17:14.802
We don't want to spend time on hypotheses
that have already been proven wrong

00:17:14.802 --> 00:17:16.887
even when people just
want to stick with them.

00:17:16.887 --> 00:17:21.015
It's just a waste of time exactly as you
said to time that we will never get back.

00:17:21.015 --> 00:17:23.040
And so, Ruth, I have one more question for

00:17:23.040 --> 00:17:25.247
you that I would like to
have your thoughts on.

00:17:25.247 --> 00:17:29.576
So sometimes people argue with important
decisions that they want to have a red

00:17:29.576 --> 00:17:34.457
team and blue team where you intentionally
assign people to take opposing positions.

00:17:34.457 --> 00:17:35.883
Have you ever tried this?

00:17:35.883 --> 00:17:38.311
Do you think that this is
something that would work well?

00:17:38.311 --> 00:17:40.808
Under what conditions do you
think it will work well, and

00:17:40.808 --> 00:17:42.800
when do you think it
would not work very well?

00:17:42.800 --> 00:17:46.910
&gt;&gt; I actually use that quite often,
I think it's a great idea.

00:17:46.910 --> 00:17:50.876
I think that when business leaders are
putting forth the proposal, they're not

00:17:50.876 --> 00:17:54.971
putting it forth because they think it's
a bad idea, they think it's a good idea.

00:17:54.971 --> 00:17:57.214
So what I want to hear is the what can
go wrong or why would I not do this?

00:17:57.214 --> 00:18:00.405
How much consensus was there on the idea?

00:18:00.405 --> 00:18:04.336
What were the voices that
were actually questioning it?

00:18:04.336 --> 00:18:08.408
And I think there are multiple ways
to get at that kind of debate.

00:18:08.408 --> 00:18:12.060
There are plenty of times where I'll say,
I want somebody to take the alternate

00:18:12.060 --> 00:18:14.415
view, walk me through it,
why would I not do this?

00:18:14.415 --> 00:18:19.297
But I would say that in an even
more basic level in meetings,

00:18:19.297 --> 00:18:21.943
I think it's extremely value.

00:18:21.943 --> 00:18:25.351
I think a best practice is, when you're
soliciting views around the room,

00:18:25.351 --> 00:18:28.778
first of all, have a diverse team
because you'll get a diversity of views.

00:18:28.778 --> 00:18:33.708
And it's one of the most potent elements
of looking at how to actually grow

00:18:33.708 --> 00:18:34.432
as a team.

00:18:34.432 --> 00:18:38.102
Second is start with the most junior
people in the room, you want to make sure

00:18:38.102 --> 00:18:41.319
you're hearing the voices of
the most junior people in the room.

00:18:41.319 --> 00:18:45.775
They're probably the ones who are most
kind of granular in the data having done

00:18:45.775 --> 00:18:46.663
the analysis.

00:18:46.663 --> 00:18:50.978
And it can be hard for a junior person to
say I actually am concerned about x, y, or

00:18:50.978 --> 00:18:54.488
z when all the senior people have
already said this is fantastic.

00:18:54.488 --> 00:18:57.975
So I think there's a process
about how you run the discussion.

00:18:57.975 --> 00:19:02.079
And then I do think at times the red team,
blue team, or team white,

00:19:02.079 --> 00:19:05.253
team whatever you want to call it,
is super helpful.

00:19:05.253 --> 00:19:06.717
So there a lot of elements and

00:19:06.717 --> 00:19:10.569
process that I think can enhance
the quality of the debate and the insight.

00:19:10.569 --> 00:19:15.801
&gt;&gt; So, Ruth, what I'm hearing from you
is that at Google, you're selected for

00:19:15.801 --> 00:19:20.341
people who appreciate the importance
of not being in a set mindset.

00:19:20.341 --> 00:19:22.829
Understand that you can be wrong, and

00:19:22.829 --> 00:19:27.434
maybe you've done something one way and
theirs might be a better way.

00:19:27.434 --> 00:19:29.100
That's not the average person.

00:19:29.100 --> 00:19:31.931
How do you maintain this culture?

00:19:31.931 --> 00:19:36.690
&gt;&gt; At Google,
I believe there's a culture of humility,

00:19:36.690 --> 00:19:40.211
people feel we're never doing enough.

00:19:40.211 --> 00:19:42.706
And sometimes that can be kind of wearing,

00:19:42.706 --> 00:19:46.045
because there's always a sense
of you're not doing enough.

00:19:46.045 --> 00:19:48.624
But in my career,
what I have seen time and

00:19:48.624 --> 00:19:53.568
time again is humility drives people to
actually raise the bar on themselves and

00:19:53.568 --> 00:19:58.624
on everything they're doing because
their concern is we're not doing enough.

00:19:58.624 --> 00:20:03.612
And I saw it with the best bankers,
I see it at Google all the time.

00:20:03.612 --> 00:20:06.717
We should be able to do more,
we should explore more,

00:20:06.717 --> 00:20:09.554
there are different things
that we can be doing.

00:20:09.554 --> 00:20:14.369
I think the key in my career
that I've seen is that

00:20:14.369 --> 00:20:19.310
the single best predictor for
failure is hubris.

00:20:19.310 --> 00:20:23.644
And it goes to so many of the things
that the two of you have been asking,

00:20:23.644 --> 00:20:28.567
which is if you want to default to gut,
you want to default to instinct, you're so

00:20:28.567 --> 00:20:30.862
smart you can throw out all the data.

00:20:30.862 --> 00:20:35.154
You don't want to engage in the debate,
that's when you need to get concerned.

00:20:35.154 --> 00:20:41.122
Because if in fact you approach things
with intellectual curiosity and

00:20:41.122 --> 00:20:46.797
humility, and it's about learning so
that you can do even better.

00:20:46.797 --> 00:20:49.495
You're going to want every
tool at your disposal, and

00:20:49.495 --> 00:20:52.819
you're going to want to be surrounded
by people who challenge you.

00:20:52.819 --> 00:20:58.280
And so, I think at the core of it,
it is about who are we individually and

00:20:58.280 --> 00:21:03.012
how do we as leaders pull together
a group that where you create

00:21:03.012 --> 00:21:08.397
the psychological safety to have
the debate, admit you don't know.

00:21:08.397 --> 00:21:12.997
But intellectual curiosity and
humility to me are absolutely

00:21:12.997 --> 00:21:17.867
critical if you want to build
a successful high performance team.

00:21:17.867 --> 00:21:19.514
&gt;&gt; I couldn't agree more.

00:21:19.514 --> 00:21:24.056
The idea of humility, understanding
that you might not know the answer,

00:21:24.056 --> 00:21:26.922
that you always need to learn is so
important.

00:21:26.922 --> 00:21:32.291
It's one of the reasons Joseph and I were
motivated to create this podcast series.

00:21:32.291 --> 00:21:36.910
&gt;&gt; Yeah, well, it's to acknowledge
to be in a leadership position,

00:21:36.910 --> 00:21:40.158
say I don't know, for
many people it's hard.

00:21:40.158 --> 00:21:43.022
I actually think that
level of openness and

00:21:43.022 --> 00:21:46.291
vulnerability actually
build stronger teams.

00:21:46.291 --> 00:21:48.642
Your team is want to know
that their voices wanted.

00:21:48.642 --> 00:21:53.539
I often tell people, we don't hire super
talented people and not want to hear what

00:21:53.539 --> 00:21:58.022
they say, the reason you're at the table
is we want to know what you think.

00:21:58.022 --> 00:22:02.327
And so, I think that the leader knows
everything isn't going to get the best

00:22:02.327 --> 00:22:05.674
other team and they're not
going to have the best results.

00:22:05.674 --> 00:22:08.084
&gt;&gt; Thank you so much,
Ruth, this was just great.

00:22:08.084 --> 00:22:11.418
We really appreciate the opportunity
to talk with you today,

00:22:11.418 --> 00:22:13.479
it's been a pleasure talking to you.

00:22:13.479 --> 00:22:17.784
Thank you so much for finding the time and
sharing your insights with us.

00:22:17.784 --> 00:22:20.066
&gt;&gt; Thank you, as well,
I hope see you out the view,

00:22:20.066 --> 00:22:23.256
I look forward to staying in touch and
seeing where you go with this.

00:22:23.256 --> 00:22:25.637
Thank you, bye bye.

00:22:25.637 --> 00:22:29.913
&gt;&gt; Wow, that was such a great interview,
and she had so many good points to make.

00:22:29.913 --> 00:22:33.747
Also related to the things that we talk
about are students in class all the time.

00:22:33.747 --> 00:22:37.893
Ultimately, this is what this
podcast is going to be all about,

00:22:37.893 --> 00:22:41.816
smart thinking, concrete examples,
and great guests.

00:22:41.816 --> 00:22:45.556
But more particularly,
it's going to be about making mistakes,

00:22:45.556 --> 00:22:49.854
identifying those mistakes, and
understanding why those mistakes were

00:22:49.854 --> 00:22:54.167
made to begin with, because that allows
us to learn from those mistakes.

00:22:54.167 --> 00:22:58.158
&gt;&gt; But the key insight is unless you're
willing to admit you've made a mistake

00:22:58.158 --> 00:23:02.603
in the first place, we can't even begin
the process of learning from our mistakes.

00:23:02.603 --> 00:23:08.606
And I think today we saw the importance
of, a, admitting that we make mistakes,

00:23:08.606 --> 00:23:13.461
but b, understanding that that is
a difficult process to do, and

00:23:13.461 --> 00:23:16.045
then going through that process.

00:23:16.045 --> 00:23:20.917
In fact, I would say another way around,
the only way of becoming a better

00:23:20.917 --> 00:23:25.485
business decision maker is to be
willing to go through that process.

00:23:25.485 --> 00:23:27.125
&gt;&gt; So in our next episode,

00:23:27.125 --> 00:23:31.965
we're going to explore a very important
mistake that many people make.

00:23:31.965 --> 00:23:35.026
And in the process we will also
explain why we decided to call this

00:23:35.026 --> 00:23:36.226
podcast All Else Equal.

